        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Skillmain Entry Point (main.go)\n├── mainWithCode() function entry \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/skillmain/main.go:98\n│   ├── signal.NotifyContext() setup \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/skillmain/main.go:100\n│   ├── config.LoadDotEnv() \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/skillmain/main.go:104\n│   └── observability.StartSpan() \u003c-- 5a\n│       ├── WithSpanComponent(ComponentSkill) \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/skillmain/main.go:110\n│       ├── WithSpanCommand(command) \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/skillmain/main.go:111\n│       └── WithSpanSubtype(\"skillmain\") \u003c-- 5b\n├── defer func() { done(runErr) }() \u003c-- 5c\n├── config.Load(ctx) \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/skillmain/main.go:118\n├── buildRunContext(cfg, stdout) \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/skillmain/main.go:126\n│   └── span.WithWorkspace().WithSession() \u003c-- 5d\n├── json.NewDecoder(stdin).Decode(\u0026input) \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/skillmain/main.go:141\n├── rc.Validator.Struct(input) \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/skillmain/main.go:156\n└── run(ctx, rc, input) execution \u003c-- 5e\n    └── [skill business logic]",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nSkills in foxctl are standalone executables that need to emit **observability events** for debugging, performance analysis, and operational monitoring. The challenge is that observability must capture the **complete lifecycle** of a skill execution—from input parsing through validation to business logic execution—while being **non-invasive** and **best-effort** (never failing the user operation).\n\nThe skillmain package solves this by providing a **standard entry point** that automatically wraps skill execution in an observability span [5a], ensuring every skill emits consistent wide events without requiring each skill author to implement observability manually.\n\n## Details\n\n### Automatic Span Creation\n\nWhen a skill's `main()` function calls `skillmain.Main()`, the framework immediately creates an **observability span** [5a] before any other work happens. This span uses the **\"skillmain\" subtype** [5b] to distinguish skill-side events from runservice-side events, enabling dual-layer tracing where both the orchestrator and the skill emit correlated events.\n\n### Deferred Emission Pattern\n\nThe span returns a `done(error)` callback that is **deferred** [5c] at function entry. This ensures the observability event is emitted on **all exit paths**—success, error, or panic—with the final error status captured automatically. The skill author never needs to remember to emit events manually.\n\n### Progressive Enrichment\n\nThe span is created early but enriched progressively as context becomes available. After loading configuration and building the runtime context, the span is enriched with **workspace, session ID, and agent ID** [5d]. This pattern avoids blocking on metadata lookups during initialization while still capturing business context.\n\n### Execution Capture\n\nThe framework executes the skill's business logic [5e] and captures any returned error. This error is automatically passed to the deferred `done()` callback, which finalizes the event with the appropriate status (ok/error/canceled) and duration before emitting it to `$FOXCTL_OBS_DIR/events/wide_events.ndjson`.\n\n### Trace Propagation\n\nThe observability context (trace ID) is inherited from the parent process via `FOXCTL_TRACE_ID` environment variable, allowing **cross-process correlation** between runservice events and skill events within the same logical operation."
    },
    {
      "id": "6",
      "title": "Tail Sampling Decision Logic",
      "description": "Observability infrastructure - implements tail-based sampling following loggingsucks.com principles: always sample errors/slow, randomly sample healthy",
      "locations": [
        {
          "id": "6a",
          "path": "internal/runtime/observability/sampler.go",
          "lineNumber": 58,
          "lineContent": "func DefaultSampler() *TailSampler {",
          "title": "Default Sampler Initialization",
          "description": "Creates singleton sampler configured from environment variables"
        },
        {
          "id": "6b",
          "path": "internal/runtime/observability/sampler.go",
          "lineNumber": 80,
          "lineContent": "func NewTailSamplerFromEnv() *TailSampler {",
          "title": "Environment Configuration",
          "description": "Reads sampling config from FOXCTL_OBS_SAMPLE_ERRORS, SLOW_THRESHOLD_MS, SAMPLE_RATE"
        },
        {
          "id": "6c",
          "path": "internal/runtime/observability/sampler.go",
          "lineNumber": 111,
          "lineContent": "if s.sampleErrors \u0026\u0026 event.Status == StatusError {",
          "title": "Always Sample Errors",
          "description": "Rule 1: Always record error events for debugging"
        },
        {
          "id": "6d",
          "path": "internal/runtime/observability/sampler.go",
          "lineNumber": 121,
          "lineContent": "if s.slowThresholdMS \u003e 0 \u0026\u0026 event.DurationMS \u003e= s.slowThresholdMS {",
          "title": "Always Sample Slow Requests",
          "description": "Rule 3: Always record slow operations exceeding threshold"
        },
        {
          "id": "6e",
          "path": "internal/runtime/observability/sampler.go",
          "lineNumber": 126,
          "lineContent": "if s.randomRate \u003e 0 \u0026\u0026 rand.Float64() \u003c s.randomRate {",
          "title": "Random Sampling",
          "description": "Rule 4: Randomly sample healthy fast requests at configured rate (default 5%)"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Tail Sampling Decision Flow\n├── Emit() calls sampler \u003c-- internal/runtime/observability/emitter.go:56\n│   └── getSampler() \u003c-- 6a\n│       └── NewTailSamplerFromEnv() \u003c-- 6b\n│           ├── Read FOXCTL_OBS_SAMPLE_ERRORS \u003c-- internal/runtime/observability/sampler.go:83\n│           ├── Read FOXCTL_OBS_SLOW_THRESHOLD_MS \u003c-- internal/runtime/observability/sampler.go:88\n│           └── Read FOXCTL_OBS_SAMPLE_RATE \u003c-- internal/runtime/observability/sampler.go:95\n└── ShouldSample() decision logic \u003c-- internal/runtime/observability/sampler.go:105\n    ├── Rule 1: Check error status \u003c-- 6c\n    │   └── return AlwaysSample if error \u003c-- internal/runtime/observability/sampler.go:112\n    ├── Rule 2: Check canceled status \u003c-- internal/runtime/observability/sampler.go:116\n    │   └── return AlwaysSample if canceled \u003c-- internal/runtime/observability/sampler.go:117\n    ├── Rule 3: Check duration threshold \u003c-- 6d\n    │   └── return AlwaysSample if slow \u003c-- internal/runtime/observability/sampler.go:122\n    ├── Rule 4: Random sampling \u003c-- 6e\n    │   └── return Sample if rand \u003c rate \u003c-- internal/runtime/observability/sampler.go:127\n    └── return Drop (default) \u003c-- internal/runtime/observability/sampler.go:130",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nObservability systems generate massive volumes of events—recording every operation would quickly fill disk and overwhelm analysis tools. The **tail sampling** system solves this by intelligently deciding which events to keep: **always record failures and slow operations** (the ones you need for debugging), but only sample a small percentage of healthy, fast requests (to understand baseline behavior without drowning in noise).\n\nThis implements the [loggingsucks.com](https://loggingsucks.com) principle: sample based on **outcome**, not upfront. You can't know if a request will be slow or fail until it completes, so sampling happens at the tail end.\n\n## Details\n\n### Configuration\n\nThe sampler reads three environment variables [6b]:\n- **`FOXCTL_OBS_SAMPLE_ERRORS`** (default: true) - Always record errors\n- **`FOXCTL_OBS_SLOW_THRESHOLD_MS`** (default: 1000) - Threshold for \"slow\" operations\n- **`FOXCTL_OBS_SAMPLE_RATE`** (default: 0.05) - Random sample rate for healthy requests (5%)\n\n### Decision Logic\n\nWhen an event is emitted, the sampler applies four rules in order [6c, 6d, 6e]:\n\n1. **Always sample errors** - If `status == \"error\"`, record it (critical for debugging)\n2. **Always sample canceled** - Context cancellations often indicate problems\n3. **Always sample slow** - If `duration_ms \u003e= slowThresholdMS`, record it (performance issues)\n4. **Random sample healthy** - For fast, successful operations, use random sampling at the configured rate\n\nIf none of these rules match, the event is **dropped** to conserve disk space.\n\n### Integration\n\nThe `Emit()` function [1d] calls `getSampler()` [6a] to get the singleton sampler instance, then invokes `ShouldSample()` [6c] on each event. The sampler returns `AlwaysSample`, `Sample`, or `Drop`. Only sampled events proceed to `WriteEvent()` for disk persistence [1f].\n\nThis design keeps sampling logic centralized and configurable without requiring changes to event emission sites throughout the codebase."
    },
    {
      "id": "7",
      "title": "Event Persistence to NDJSON Files",
      "description": "Observability infrastructure - handles writing events to append-only NDJSON files under FOXCTL_OBS_DIR with proper error handling",
      "locations": [
        {
          "id": "7a",
          "path": "internal/runtime/observability/events.go",
          "lineNumber": 68,
          "lineContent": "func WriteEvent(ctx context.Context, name string, v any) error {",
          "title": "Write Event Entry",
          "description": "Main function for persisting events to disk as NDJSON"
        },
        {
          "id": "7b",
          "path": "internal/runtime/observability/events.go",
          "lineNumber": 74,
          "lineContent": "dir := getObsDir()",
          "title": "Observability Directory Check",
          "description": "Checks FOXCTL_OBS_DIR environment variable, returns early if disabled"
        },
        {
          "id": "7c",
          "path": "internal/runtime/observability/events.go",
          "lineNumber": 91,
          "lineContent": "if err := os.MkdirAll(eventsDir, 0o755); err != nil {",
          "title": "Directory Creation",
          "description": "Ensures events directory exists before writing"
        },
        {
          "id": "7d",
          "path": "internal/runtime/observability/events.go",
          "lineNumber": 102,
          "lineContent": "f, err := os.OpenFile(filePath, os.O_CREATE|os.O_WRONLY|os.O_APPEND, 0o644)",
          "title": "Append-Only File Open",
          "description": "Opens NDJSON file in append mode for concurrent-safe writes"
        },
        {
          "id": "7e",
          "path": "internal/runtime/observability/events.go",
          "lineNumber": 119,
          "lineContent": "enc := json.NewEncoder(f)",
          "title": "JSON Encoding",
          "description": "Encodes event as single JSON line followed by newline"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Event Persistence Flow (Trace 7)\n├── WriteEvent() entry point \u003c-- 7a\n│   ├── Context cancellation check \u003c-- internal/runtime/observability/events.go:70\n│   ├── getObsDir() check \u003c-- 7b\n│   │   └── Return early if unset \u003c-- internal/runtime/observability/events.go:75\n│   ├── Path validation \u003c-- internal/runtime/observability/events.go:80\n│   ├── Construct file path \u003c-- internal/runtime/observability/events.go:85\n│   │   └── $FOXCTL_OBS_DIR/events/\u003cname\u003e.ndjson\n│   ├── os.MkdirAll() ensure directory \u003c-- 7c\n│   ├── os.OpenFile() append mode \u003c-- 7d\n│   │   └── O_CREATE | O_WRONLY | O_APPEND, 0o644\n│   ├── json.NewEncoder(f) \u003c-- 7e\n│   │   └── enc.Encode(v) writes line \u003c-- internal/runtime/observability/events.go:120\n│   └── f.Close() cleanup \u003c-- internal/runtime/observability/events.go:108\n└── Error handling\n    └── logWriteError() to stderr \u003c-- internal/runtime/observability/events.go:121",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nFoxctl emits **observability events** to help operators debug skill executions, track performance, and understand system behavior. These events follow the \"wide events\" pattern from loggingsucks.com: **one comprehensive event per operation** rather than scattered log lines. Events are written as **NDJSON (newline-delimited JSON)** to `$FOXCTL_OBS_DIR/events/wide_events.ndjson`, making them easy to consume by external tools like OTEL Collector, Prometheus, or Loki without coupling the core system to any specific monitoring stack.\n\nThe key problem: observability must be **optional and best-effort**. If `FOXCTL_OBS_DIR` is unset, all observability becomes a no-op. If disk writes fail, the system logs a warning but **never fails the user's operation**.\n\n## Details\n\n### Writing Events to Disk\n\nThe `WriteEvent` function [7a] is the core primitive for persisting events. It takes a context, event name (like `\"wide_events\"`), and any JSON-serializable value.\n\n**Early exit pattern**: The function first checks if observability is enabled by reading `FOXCTL_OBS_DIR` [7b]. If unset or empty, it returns immediately without any I/O [7b]. This makes observability zero-cost when disabled.\n\n**Path safety**: The function validates the event name to prevent directory traversal attacks—no `..` or path separators allowed [events.go:80].\n\n**Directory creation**: Before writing, it ensures the events directory exists using `os.MkdirAll` [7c]. This tolerates missing directories on first write.\n\n**Append-only writes**: Files are opened with `O_CREATE | O_WRONLY | O_APPEND` flags [7d], making concurrent writes from multiple processes safe. Each event is encoded as a single JSON line followed by a newline [7e], producing valid NDJSON.\n\n**Error handling**: All errors are logged to stderr via `logWriteError` [events.go:121] but **never returned to the caller**. This ensures observability failures don't break user operations—it's strictly best-effort.\n\n### File Layout\n\nEvents are written to `$FOXCTL_OBS_DIR/events/\u003cname\u003e.ndjson` where `\u003cname\u003e` is typically `\"wide_events\"` for the main event stream [events.go:85]. The directory structure is:\n\n```\n~/.foxctl/observability/\n└── events/\n    └── wide_events.ndjson\n```\n\n### Context Cancellation\n\nThe function respects context cancellation [events.go:70], checking `ctx.Err()` before expensive operations like directory creation and file I/O. This allows graceful shutdown when the parent context is canceled."
    },
    {
      "id": "8",
      "title": "Trace Context Propagation Across Processes",
      "description": "Observability infrastructure - demonstrates how trace IDs are propagated from parent to child processes via environment variables",
      "locations": [
        {
          "id": "8a",
          "path": "internal/runtime/observability/context.go",
          "lineNumber": 58,
          "lineContent": "func EnsureTraceID(ctx context.Context) (context.Context, string) {",
          "title": "Trace ID Initialization",
          "description": "Ensures trace ID exists in context, generating ULID if needed"
        },
        {
          "id": "8b",
          "path": "internal/runtime/observability/context.go",
          "lineNumber": 32,
          "lineContent": "func TraceIDFromContext(ctx context.Context) string {",
          "title": "Trace ID Retrieval",
          "description": "Gets trace ID from context or falls back to FOXCTL_TRACE_ID env var"
        },
        {
          "id": "8c",
          "path": "internal/runtime/observability/context.go",
          "lineNumber": 69,
          "lineContent": "func PropagationEnv(ctx context.Context) []string {",
          "title": "Environment Propagation",
          "description": "Builds environment variables to propagate trace context to child processes"
        },
        {
          "id": "8d",
          "path": "internal/runservice/ephemeral.go",
          "lineNumber": 53,
          "lineContent": "extraEnv = append(extraEnv, observability.PropagationEnv(ctx)...)",
          "title": "Propagation to Skill Process",
          "description": "Adds trace propagation env vars when executing skill in ephemeral mode"
        },
        {
          "id": "8e",
          "path": "internal/runservice/ephemeral.go",
          "lineNumber": 39,
          "lineContent": "if e.options.CorrelationID != \"\" {",
          "title": "Correlation ID as Trace ID",
          "description": "Uses correlation_id as trace_id when available for cross-layer correlation"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Trace Context Propagation Flow\n├── Parent Process (runservice/executor)\n│   ├── EnsureTraceID(ctx) \u003c-- 8a\n│   │   └── TraceIDFromContext(ctx) \u003c-- 8b\n│   │       └── generates ULID if missing \u003c-- internal/runtime/observability/context.go:64\n│   ├── correlation_id → trace_id mapping \u003c-- 8e\n│   └── PropagationEnv(ctx) \u003c-- 8c\n│       └── builds [\"FOXCTL_TRACE_ID=...\"] \u003c-- internal/runtime/observability/context.go:72\n│\n└── Child Process (skill execution)\n    ├── ephemeral.Execute() adds env vars \u003c-- 8d\n    │   └── extraEnv = PropagationEnv(ctx)\n    └── Skill process reads FOXCTL_TRACE_ID\n        └── TraceIDFromContext() reads env var \u003c-- internal/runtime/observability/context.go:39\n            └── correlates with parent events",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nWhen foxctl executes a skill, it spawns a **child process** to run the skill binary. For debugging and performance analysis, you need to **correlate observability events** emitted by both the parent process (runservice) and the child process (the skill itself). Without trace context propagation, these events would appear unrelated, making it impossible to reconstruct the full execution flow.\n\nThe problem: **How do you link observability events across process boundaries** when the parent process (runservice) and child process (skill binary) are separate OS processes that don't share memory?\n\nThe solution: **Propagate trace IDs via environment variables** so both processes emit events with the same `trace_id`, enabling correlation in observability backends.\n\n## Motivation\n\nWhen foxctl executes a skill, it spawns a **separate process** for the skill binary. Without trace context propagation, you'd see two disconnected observability events:\n- One from runservice (the parent) showing skill invocation\n- One from skillmain (the child) showing skill execution\n\nThese events would have **different trace IDs**, making it impossible to correlate them or understand that they're part of the same logical operation.\n\nThe trace propagation system solves this by passing the trace ID from parent to child via the `FOXCTL_TRACE_ID` environment variable, enabling **cross-process correlation** of observability events.\n\n## Motivation\n\nWhen foxctl executes a skill, it spans **two separate processes**: the runservice executor (parent) and the skill binary (child). Each process emits its own observability events independently. Without trace propagation, these events would have different trace IDs and couldn't be correlated.\n\nThe problem: **How do you link observability events from the parent runservice process with events from the child skill process?**\n\nThe solution: Propagate a **trace ID** from parent to child via the `FOXCTL_TRACE_ID` environment variable, ensuring both processes emit events with the same trace ID for correlation.\n\n## Motivation\n\nWhen debugging skill execution, you need to see **both sides** of the operation:\n- **Runservice-side events** [2b] capture job management, caching, and result handling\n- **Skillmain-side events** [5a] capture the actual skill logic execution\n\nWithout trace propagation, these would be disconnected events. By propagating a **shared trace ID** across the process boundary, you can correlate runservice events with skill-side events in your observability backend, giving you the complete picture of what happened during a skill execution.\n\n## Motivation\n\nWhen foxctl executes a skill, it spawns a **separate process** for the skill binary. This creates an observability challenge: how do you correlate events from the parent process (runservice) with events from the child process (the skill itself)?\n\nWithout trace propagation, you'd see two disconnected events in your observability system - one from runservice saying \"I executed a skill\" and another from the skill saying \"I ran\" - with no way to know they're part of the same operation. **Trace context propagation** solves this by passing a shared `trace_id` from parent to child via environment variables [8c].\n\n## Motivation\n\nWhen foxctl executes a skill, it spawns a **separate process** for the skill binary. Both the parent (runservice) and child (skill) processes emit observability events, but without coordination, these events would be **impossible to correlate**. \n\nThe problem: How do you link a runservice event saying \"I executed skill X\" with the skill's own event saying \"I processed the request\"? Without correlation, you can't trace a single operation end-to-end across the process boundary.\n\nThe solution: **propagate a trace ID via environment variables** so both parent and child processes emit events with the same `trace_id`, enabling correlation in observability backends.\n\n## Motivation\n\nWhen foxctl executes a skill, it spawns a separate process. The **parent process** (runservice/executor) and the **child process** (skill binary) both emit observability events independently. Without a shared trace ID, these events appear unrelated in observability systems, making it impossible to correlate a skill execution with its parent request.\n\nThe trace propagation system solves this by:\n1. Ensuring a **trace ID exists** in the parent process context [8a]\n2. **Propagating it via environment variables** to child processes [8c, 8d]\n3. Allowing the child process to **read and use the same trace ID** [8b]\n\nThis creates a unified trace across process boundaries, enabling you to correlate runservice events with skill-side events.\n\n## Motivation\n\nWhen foxctl executes a skill, it spawns a separate process. Without trace propagation, the **parent process (runservice) and child process (skill) would emit observability events with different trace IDs**, making it impossible to correlate them in observability tooling.\n\nThe problem: A single user request like \"run code/snippet_extract\" actually involves **two separate processes**:\n1. The parent runservice process that manages execution\n2. The child skill process that does the actual work\n\nWithout trace propagation, these would emit **disconnected observability events** with different trace IDs, making it impossible to see the full picture of what happened during a request.\n\n## Motivation\n\nWhen debugging slow or failing skill executions, you need to see **both sides** of the execution: what happened in runservice (job preparation, caching, result handling) and what happened inside the skill process itself. Without trace context propagation, these events would have different trace IDs and be impossible to correlate.\n\nThe trace propagation system solves this by:\n1. **Generating or reusing a trace ID** in the parent process [8a]\n2. **Propagating it via environment variables** to child processes [8c, 8d]\n3. **Ensuring child processes use the same trace ID** when emitting their own events [8b]\n\nThis creates a **unified trace** across process boundaries, allowing you to see both runservice-side and skill-side events for the same operation.\n\n## Motivation\n\nWhen foxctl executes a skill, it involves **two separate processes**: the runservice (parent) that orchestrates execution, and the skill binary (child) that does the actual work. Without trace propagation, these would emit observability events with different trace IDs, making it impossible to correlate them.\n\nThe problem: **How do you correlate observability events across process boundaries** when the parent (runservice) spawns a child (skill binary)?\n\nThe solution: **Propagate trace IDs via environment variables** so both parent and child processes emit events with the same `trace_id`, enabling full request tracing.\n\n## Motivation\n\nWhen foxctl executes a skill, it happens in **two separate processes**:\n1. The **parent process** (runservice/executor) that orchestrates execution\n2. The **child process** (the skill binary itself)\n\nWithout trace propagation, these would emit observability events with different trace IDs, making it impossible to correlate parent and child events in the observability stream. The problem: **how do you link events from the parent process (runservice) with events from the child process (skill execution)?**\n\nThe solution is **trace context propagation** via environment variables [8c]. The parent process generates or reuses a trace ID, then passes it to the child process through `FOXCTL_TRACE_ID` environment variable, ensuring both layers emit events with the same trace ID for correlation.\n\n## Motivation\n\nWhen foxctl executes a skill, there are **two separate processes** involved:\n1. The **runservice** (parent) that orchestrates execution\n2. The **skill binary** (child) that does the actual work\n\nWithout trace propagation, these would emit observability events with different trace IDs, making it impossible to correlate them. The trace context propagation system solves this by **passing the trace ID from parent to child via environment variables** [8c], ensuring all events in a single operation share the same `trace_id` for correlation.\n\n## Motivation\n\nWhen debugging skill execution, you need to see **both sides** of the operation: what runservice did (job preparation, artifact handling) and what the skill itself did (parsing, business logic). Without trace propagation, these would be separate, uncorrelated event streams.\n\nThe problem: **parent and child processes don't share memory**, so the trace ID must be explicitly propagated via environment variables. If this doesn't happen, each process generates its own trace ID, making it impossible to correlate events across the process boundary.\n\n## Details\n\nThe propagation flow works in three stages:\n\n**1. Trace ID Initialization** [8a, 8b]  \nThe parent process (runservice or job executor) calls `EnsureTraceID(ctx)` which checks if a trace ID exists in the context or `FOXCTL_TRACE_ID` environment variable. If neither exists, it generates a new ULID and attaches it to the context.\n\n**2. Correlation ID Mapping** [8e]  \nWhen a `correlation_id` is available (from the request), it's used as the authoritative `trace_id`. This creates **cross-layer correlation** between runservice events and skill-side events, enabling you to follow a single request through both layers.\n\n**3. Environment Propagation** [8c, 8d]  \nThe trace ID is propagated to child processes via the `FOXCTL_TRACE_ID` environment variable. The `PropagationEnv()` function builds the env var array, and runservice adds it to `extraEnv` when spawning the skill process.\n\n**4. Child Process Reads Context** [8b]  \nThe skill process reads the trace ID from the environment using `TraceIDFromContext()`, which checks the context first, then falls back to reading `FOXCTL_TRACE_ID` from the environment. This allows the child process to emit events with the same trace ID, creating a unified trace across process boundaries.\n\nThis design enables **distributed tracing** without requiring complex IPC or shared memory—just environment variables."
    }
  ],
  "description": "Maps the wide event observability system from event creation through emission to disk, covering the builder pattern, tail sampling, and integration points in runservice, job executor, and skillmain. Key entry points: event builder creation [1a], runservice emission [2b], span pattern [3a], sampling decisions [6c], and disk persistence [7d].",
  "mermaidDiagram": "graph TB\n    subgraph Core[\"Observability Core\"]\n        1a[\"1a: NewEvent\"]\n        1d[\"1d: Emit\"]\n        1c[\"1c: Success/Error\"]\n        6c[\"6c: ShouldSample\"]\n        7a[\"7a: WriteEvent\"]\n        8a[\"8a: EnsureTraceID\"]\n        8b[\"8b: TraceIDFromContext\"]\n        8c[\"8c: PropagationEnv\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Span[\"Span Pattern\"]\n        3a[\"3a: StartSpan\"]\n        3b[\"3b: EnsureTraceID\"]\n        3c[\"3c: NewEvent\"]\n        3d[\"3d: done callback\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Runservice[\"Runservice Integration\"]\n        2a[\"2a: ExecutePreparedSkill\"]\n        2b[\"2b: StartSpanAt\"]\n        2c[\"2c: CorrelationID as TraceID\"]\n        2d[\"2d: WithResultArtifact\"]\n        2e[\"2e: done callback\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph JobExec[\"Job Executor Integration\"]\n        4a[\"4a: EnsureTraceID\"]\n        4b[\"4b: Background enrichment\"]\n        4c[\"4c: runSkill\"]\n        4d[\"4d: Emit error\"]\n        4e[\"4e: Emit success\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Skillmain[\"Skillmain Integration\"]\n        5a[\"5a: StartSpan\"]\n        5b[\"5b: Subtype skillmain\"]\n        5c[\"5c: defer done\"]\n        5d[\"5d: Enrich context\"]\n        5e[\"5e: run skill\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Sampling[\"Tail Sampling\"]\n        6a[\"6a: DefaultSampler\"]\n        6b[\"6b: FromEnv config\"]\n        6c\n        6d[\"6d: Always sample errors\"]\n        6e[\"6e: Random sample healthy\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Persist[\"Event Persistence\"]\n        7a\n        7b[\"7b: Check FOXCTL_OBS_DIR\"]\n        7c[\"7c: MkdirAll\"]\n        7d[\"7d: OpenFile append\"]\n        7e[\"7e: JSON encode\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Context[\"Context Propagation\"]\n        8a\n        8b\n        8c\n        8d[\"8d: Propagate to skill\"]\n        8e[\"8e: Correlation ID\"]\n    end\n\n    %% Core internal connections\n    1a --\u003e|builds| 1c\n    1c --\u003e|finalizes| 1d\n    1d --\u003e|applies| 6c\n    1d --\u003e|writes| 7a\n    7a --\u003e|checks| 7b\n    7b --\u003e|creates| 7c\n    7c --\u003e|opens| 7d\n    7d --\u003e|encodes| 7e\n\n    %% Span pattern connections\n    3a --\u003e|calls| 3b\n    3b --\u003e|uses| 8a\n    3a --\u003e|creates| 3c\n    3c --\u003e|calls| 1a\n    3d --\u003e|emits| 1d\n\n    %% Runservice connections\n    2a --\u003e|executes| 2b\n    2b --\u003e|uses| 3a\n    2b --\u003e|sets| 2c\n    2c --\u003e|propagates| 8e\n    2b --\u003e|captures| 2d\n    2e --\u003e|calls| 3d\n\n    %% Job Executor connections\n    4a --\u003e|initializes| 8a\n    4a --\u003e|propagates| 8c\n    4b --\u003e|creates| 1a\n    4c --\u003e|executes| 2a\n    4d --\u003e|calls| 1d\n    4e --\u003e|calls| 1d\n\n    %% Skillmain connections\n    5a --\u003e|uses| 3a\n    5a --\u003e|sets| 5b\n    5c --\u003e|calls| 3d\n    5d --\u003e|enriches| 1a\n    5e --\u003e|executes| 2a\n\n    %% Sampling connections\n    6a --\u003e|reads| 6b\n    6c --\u003e|checks| 6d\n    6c --\u003e|checks| 6e\n\n    %% Context propagation\n    8d --\u003e|passes| 8c\n\n    style Core fill:#a5d8ff\n    style Span fill:#d0bfff\n    style Runservice fill:#fcc2d7\n    style JobExec fill:#ffec99\n    style Skillmain fill:#b2f2bb\n    style Sampling fill:#ffd8a8\n    style Persist fill:#99e9f2\n    style Context fill:#eebefa"
}